WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.801
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:03.402
is an Emmy-nominated actress
you know

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:04.938
from her work
on television shows

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.573
such as "Friends"
and "Cougar Town."

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:08.173
She both produces and stars

00:00:08.173 --> 00:00:09.475
in the new series
"Shining Vale,"

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:11.344
which premieres
this Sunday on Starz.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.412
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:16.248
-You look nice.

00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:18.151
-And...where's the insult?

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:19.219
-No insult.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:20.753
That dress hides your flass.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:22.354
-And that's mean slang for...?

00:00:22.354 --> 00:00:23.289
-Flat ass, obviously.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.357
-Oh, obviously.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:25.558
You look nice, too.

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:26.859
Is that for your
virgin boyfriend

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:29.629
or is today the big
Diane Sawyer costume contest?

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:31.197
-I don't get that
old person reference,

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.464
but, yes, this is for Ryan.

00:00:32.464 --> 00:00:34.533
-Surprisingly respectful.

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:36.335
-Yeah, I'm trying to break him,
but his Lord and Savior

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:37.736
continues to [bleep]block me.

00:00:37.736 --> 00:00:39.939
-Aw, that's my baby girl.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:40.839
[ Laughter ]
Please welcome to the show

00:00:40.839 --> 00:00:42.475
Courteney Cox, everybody.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:51.417 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:57.490 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.692
Welcome to the show.
-Thank you.

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:01.193
-I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:03.062
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.931
-So, obviously,
that is your TV daughter,

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:07.299
who is about the same age

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:09.536
as your real-life daughter,
Coco.

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.438
Does that make it easier
to act like the mother

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.808
of a 17-year-old daughter,
when you actually have one?

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.809
-Well, I can really relate.

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:18.244
This character,

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.379
played by Gus Birney,
who is incredible,

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:22.948
is a little more relentless
than Coco.

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:25.118
-That's good.
-But, I mean...

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:26.319
[ Laughter ]
...you could see how

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:27.620
-That was pretty relentless.
-snappy she is.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.888
[ Laughter ]
That was pretty relentless.

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.756
And, you know, I don't think
this is great parenting,

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:35.060
but I can't help but sometimes
just like I imitate her.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.329
[ Laughter ]
I mean, she's a little brat?

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.330
Guess what -- so am I, yeah.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:39.532
[ Laughter ]
-Is that something you do

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:41.367
as a real-life parent,
do you imitate?

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.968
-That's what I do,
and I don't mean to,

00:01:42.968 --> 00:01:44.070
but, if she's sassy,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.037
then, you know, I don't know.

00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:46.171
I just can't help it.

00:01:46.171 --> 00:01:47.706
I go back to that age.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:49.575
[ Laughter ]
-I'm finding I'm doing it --

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:50.709
My kids are younger, obviously,

00:01:50.709 --> 00:01:53.145
but, when my
five-year-old whines,

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:54.814
when I know he's just whining
and he's like,

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.482
"I don't want to read
that book," I'm like,

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:57.950
"Oh, you don't?!"

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.519
[ Laughter ]
-I do that.
-And my wife says,

00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:02.187
"What do you think
you're accomplishing?

00:02:02.187 --> 00:02:03.489
[ Laughter ]
What do you think?

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.424
What was the endgame of that?"

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:08.027
-No, I completely
understand, though.

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:09.695
-I don't think there's a single
parenting book

00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:11.564
where the advice is
"Just mirror them.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.499
[ Laughter ]
Just whatever energy
they give you,

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.000
just throw it
right back at them."

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.469
-Yeah, I'm not the person
that's like,

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.171
"I can see you're upset.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.972
That must be horrible," no.

00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:21.908
[ Laughter ]
-My wife is so good at that.
-Unh-unh.

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:24.210
-My wife is always like,
"I hear that you're upset,"

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.412
and I'm like,
"We are running out of time!

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:27.514
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:29.682
The Uber doesn't care
what we hear!"

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:31.817
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.053
This show is very funny,

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:35.955
but it's also
a little bit scary.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.290
It's supernatural.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.958
You're in a home
that might have ghosts.

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:40.793
-Yeah.
-It must've been cool

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:41.994
to approach a show that you knew

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:44.130
was going to both be
laugh-out-loud funny

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.132
and also have this
horror element.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:47.400
I feel it's very unique.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.769
-Yeah, it is definitely
genre-bending.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:52.005
It's a dark comedy.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:53.706
It is very real.

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:57.075
I mean, I'm dealing
with so many issues on the show,

00:02:57.075 --> 00:02:59.411
which is why I loved
and, when I read it, I thought,

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:03.749
"I have to do this,"
but it's a family drama.

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:06.251
I have had an affair
with the handyman.

00:03:06.251 --> 00:03:07.486
My marriage is kind of

00:03:07.486 --> 00:03:08.487
falling apart, right?
-Who hasn't?

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:10.589
[ Laughter ]
-Who hasn't?

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:12.725
And we are moving
outside of the city.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:15.795
We live in New York and just
I want to start the family over

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:18.097
and, you know,
get on a new path.

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:21.467
But I'm going through menopause.
I'm depressed.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.803
I'm a writer that's had
writer's block

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:26.205
for the last like 17 years,

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:28.407
after she's written
a very racy novel,

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.309
and she has to start over.

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:33.579
But they move into this house
that is

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.114
probably, I mean, haunted.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.450
I know it's haunted,
but no one believes me.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.652
-That's the classic trope,
that there's one person

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.921
who knows it's haunted
and nobody else buys in.

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:42.822
-Exactly.

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:43.890
-In real life,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.093
did you ever buy
in to the supernatural?

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:48.461
-Well, I didn't before.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:50.295
I did live in a house
that I heard was haunted

00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:53.465
and, recently, I saw something.

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:54.634
I mean, there's no question
I saw something.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:55.735
-You saw something?!
-Yep.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:57.136
-Do you think this is because

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:58.237
the show that you were
working on

00:03:58.237 --> 00:03:59.638
planted it in your subconscious

00:03:59.638 --> 00:04:01.140
or did you fully see something?

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.041
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.643
-I really think I saw something.

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:04.710
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.445
-Was there anyone else with you

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:07.847
at the time that could verify?

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:10.616
-Yes, okay. Well, I was
with my partner, Johnny.

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:14.153
We were in the apartment
in London

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.021
and I was awake

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:20.560
and, all of a sudden,
I saw these like faces.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.594
Some of them didn't look
that welcoming.

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:24.030
[ Laughter ]
I mean, some of them looked

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.532
a little like what I would
imagine the devil to look like.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:28.734
And I was like, "Oh, [bleep]."

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:29.869
-They might've just
been British.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:31.737
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.737 --> 00:04:33.472
-They like knew you were
American and they were like --

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:34.507
[ Muttering ]

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:35.374
[ Laughter ]

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:36.509
I was thinking, "Oh, my God."

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:39.211
I was like calling out
for my mom or dad

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:41.147
and I kept saying,
these really scary ones,

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:42.348
and I thought,
"I don't want to go there,"

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:44.750
wherever they're talking about.
-Wow.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.785
-But I was actually okay.

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:48.253
The plant moved next to the bed

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:51.023
and I saw all these waves
of light and I woke up Johnny.

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:53.192
I was like, "Johnny,
you've got to see this!"

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.127
He's like, "I'm asleep."

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:56.996
He goes, "And you're asleep
right now, Courteney.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:58.197
You don't see anything."

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:01.367
I said, "No, I just woke you up.
How am I asleep?"

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.235
[ Laughter ]
And we had a big debate.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:05.772
Like the show, he didn't believe
me and I was frustrated

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:08.574
and then, yeah,
that's how it went.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:10.543
-I hope that
you should try to,

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:12.111
you know, connect with those
ghosts and be like,

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:14.080
"You guys got to go scare
the [bleep] out of Johnny."

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.815
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. I did.

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.583
The next night,
I came into the room and I said,

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:19.652
"Look, that was really
nice, guys.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.653
Had a nice meeting last night.
-Oh, this is very smart.

00:05:21.653 --> 00:05:24.122
-I cannot have it tonight.

00:05:24.122 --> 00:05:26.058
I mean, I enjoyed it."
I was trying to be really nice

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:28.527
and I don't want to be
snatched up and then taken, so,

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:30.496
I was like, "It was great.
Not again."

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:32.097
[ Laughter ]
-I haven't seen them since.
-That proves

00:05:32.097 --> 00:05:33.466
they were British ghosts
because then, they were like,

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:34.633
[ Muttering in English accent ]
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.633 --> 00:05:35.668
-[ English accent ]
I want to be polite.

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:37.035
[ Laughter ]
-[ Muttering ]

00:05:37.035 --> 00:05:38.504
-[ English accent ]
Pardon me.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:39.839
-[ English accent ]
Had no idea.

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.407
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.009
This is very exciting --

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:44.610
One of the people
you worked with on this show

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.412
is one of our favorites here,
Sharon Horgan,

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:51.617
who's a fantastic Irish writer
and comedian.

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:52.885
Were you a fan of hers
before you

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:54.287
started working on this?
-Huge fan, yeah.

00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:55.621
I mean, from "Catastrophe."

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:58.991
But you know, she created
the show with Jeff Astrof,

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:00.526
who is from "Friends."

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.027
I mean, he's done other things,
obviously,

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:05.465
but I knew him from back then
and it's a perfect combination.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:07.799
They really balance
each other out.

00:06:07.799 --> 00:06:11.537
Jeff, you know,
he writes the episodes and --

00:06:11.537 --> 00:06:13.472
But Sharon, she's so real.

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:14.807
I mean, she's just --
-She really is.

00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:17.943
-She keeps it like just her
sense of humor, it's real

00:06:17.943 --> 00:06:20.179
and Jeff is so funny
and, together,

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:22.281
I think they really
are a great combination.

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:23.482
-That's wonderful.
What a cool person

00:06:23.482 --> 00:06:24.316
to work with.
-Yeah, Jeff works really hard.

00:06:24.316 --> 00:06:28.487
I mean, this is his like baby.

00:06:28.487 --> 00:06:30.289
-You mentioned "Friends."
I don't even remember this.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:33.392
I think the last time I saw you
was about 10 years ago in L.A.

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:34.860
I was having dinner
with Amy Poehler

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.229
and you were a table over,

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:37.997
having dinner
with Jennifer Aniston.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:38.964
Do you remember this?
-Where was it?

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:40.399
-At Sunset Tower.
-Oh.

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.768
-And we were having dinner
and I just remember when I left,

00:06:42.768 --> 00:06:43.836
I said to Amy, I'm like,

00:06:43.836 --> 00:06:45.037
"People in that restaurant
were like,

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:47.172
'Hollywood is the best.

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:49.475
[ Laughter ]
All the people you see on TV

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.844
have dinner together off-camera.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:53.045
[ Laughter ]
The Friends have
dinner together.

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.714
The "Update" people
have dinner together.'"

00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:56.815
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.815 --> 00:06:58.284
You were very nice that evening.
It meant a lot.

00:06:58.284 --> 00:07:00.652
You also have your own line --

00:07:00.652 --> 00:07:02.722
Well, how would you describe it,
home goods?

00:07:02.722 --> 00:07:04.590
-Yes. I'm really,
really proud of this.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.258
I love scents.

00:07:06.258 --> 00:07:08.894
I'm obsessed with candles.

00:07:08.894 --> 00:07:13.098
But, during this lockdown,
I realized that without --

00:07:13.098 --> 00:07:14.834
You know, you're so used
to smelling Clorox,

00:07:14.834 --> 00:07:16.501
it was the most important thing
you could have.

00:07:16.501 --> 00:07:20.206
[ Laughter ]
And now, I mean,
I didn't mean to be typecast,

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:21.273
but I do like to clean.

00:07:21.273 --> 00:07:24.476
[ Laughter ]
And I like to be neat.

00:07:24.476 --> 00:07:27.179
-You can admit it now.
-Alright.

00:07:27.179 --> 00:07:30.015
So, anyway, I created this line
called Home Court

00:07:30.015 --> 00:07:33.185
and it's beauty products
for the home.

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:35.521
So, literally, you know,
you could spray your counter

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.789
with something
that really works.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:39.124
It's natural.
It's good for you.

00:07:39.124 --> 00:07:40.526
-This is a surface cleaner.
-Not good for you.

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:41.527
[ Laughter ]
It's just not bad for you.

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:43.029
Don't like in the mouth.

00:07:43.029 --> 00:07:44.329
[ Laughter ]

00:07:44.329 --> 00:07:47.032
-I want to assure you
that my audience

00:07:47.032 --> 00:07:49.635
didn't think they could eat
the cleaning product.

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:50.769
[ Laughter ]
-Okay.

00:07:50.769 --> 00:07:53.138
-This is a very well-heeled
group of people.

00:07:53.138 --> 00:07:54.807
[ Laughter ]
-I'm sorry.

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:59.578
But the scents are incredible
and it kind of makes cleaning

00:07:59.578 --> 00:08:00.913
nice and fun.
-I hope this is open

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:02.515
because I haven't cleaned
this desk in a long time.

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:04.483
-What scent do you have?
-This is Steeped Rose.

00:08:04.483 --> 00:08:06.685
-Oh, yeah. I don't like
the smell of rose,

00:08:06.685 --> 00:08:09.588
if it's too powdery,
and this is incredible.

00:08:09.588 --> 00:08:11.390
Oh, my god.
Do you not smell it and love it?

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:13.959
[ Laughter ]
-I do. I smell it and love it!
This is great.

00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:15.894
I mean, we haven't cleaned
this thing since I moved in

00:08:15.894 --> 00:08:17.195
[ Laughter ]
eight years ago.
-Let's see the rag afterwards.

00:08:17.195 --> 00:08:19.598
But it's nice, right?

00:08:19.598 --> 00:08:21.767
-The rag, it's like
nothing's on it.

00:08:21.767 --> 00:08:23.436
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.436 --> 00:08:24.570
-It works better than that.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:25.504
You've got a good crew here.

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:26.371
[ Laughter ]

00:08:26.371 --> 00:08:27.573
Oh, gross, here, get this.

00:08:27.573 --> 00:08:29.274
-Okay. Don't say gross.

00:08:29.274 --> 00:08:32.778
[ Laughter ]
-Yep.

00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:35.647
-I've heard that,
not only do you like products

00:08:35.647 --> 00:08:37.183
for the home, but you like
to have people over.

00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:38.351
You like to have
big dinner parties,

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:40.453
which, obviously,
was a thing that, you know,

00:08:40.453 --> 00:08:41.920
one of the many things
COVID took away from us,

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:43.489
was being able to host people
at our homes.

00:08:43.489 --> 00:08:44.590
-Yeah.
-Are you doing it again?

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.024
Are you having your friends
back over?

00:08:46.024 --> 00:08:47.193
-I'm doing it again.

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:50.830
I actually met Brandi Carlile
on Instagram

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:52.164
because I played one
of her songs

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:56.468
with my friend Gary Lightbody,
who, Snow Patrol.

00:08:56.468 --> 00:08:59.338
And she contacted me and said,
"Oh, you did a great job."

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:01.540
She DM'd me, which, I just
learned about Instagram

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.975
a couple of years ago.

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:05.678
-I mean, that's a great way
to learn about Instagram DMs,

00:09:05.678 --> 00:09:06.979
is from Brandi Carlile.
Well done, yeah.

00:09:06.979 --> 00:09:08.981
[ Laughter ]
-I mean, it's like a --

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:11.083
I don't want to say a dating
website because I'm not,

00:09:11.083 --> 00:09:13.518
you know, I don't do that
because I've got a partner.

00:09:13.518 --> 00:09:16.222
But it is, it's like how
you meet people, I can imagine.

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:18.724
It's better than a lot
of those other ones,

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:20.493
especially after
seeing the Tinder spoiler.

00:09:20.493 --> 00:09:22.127
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.127 --> 00:09:25.498
Anyway, what was my point?
Oh, yeah.

00:09:25.498 --> 00:09:27.533
So she was supposed to come over
on the Sunday

00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:30.168
and then that day, on whatever
day it was in March,

00:09:30.168 --> 00:09:32.805
the world shut down
and she didn't come.

00:09:32.805 --> 00:09:34.574
But afterwards,
she's been there since

00:09:34.574 --> 00:09:36.075
and played music.
-Oh, that's fantastic!

00:09:36.075 --> 00:09:37.610
-Yeah, I'm back at it.
-I think that's one

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.478
of the nice things
that people are finally doing,

00:09:39.478 --> 00:09:42.414
is like cleaning up those like
sort of unfinished plans

00:09:42.414 --> 00:09:44.082
and doing the things
they were looking forward to

00:09:44.082 --> 00:09:45.984
that were taken away.
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:45.984 --> 00:09:47.619
-Thank you.
-It's just a delight
-to have you in the studio.

00:09:47.619 --> 00:09:48.820
[ Cheering and applause ]
You guys, that's Courtney Cox.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.724
"Shining Vale" premiers on Starz
this Sunday.

